          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following correctly matches a biomolecule class with its function i</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following correctly matches a biomolecule class with its function in the cell?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nucleic acids — energy storage and cell recognition</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Energy storage and cell recognition are functions of carbohydrates. Nucleic acids carry genetic information — DNA stores it and RNA transfers it.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lipids — building membranes and carrying signals</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! Lipids serve three key roles: building cell membranes, storing energy, and carrying chemical signals. Their amphipathic nature makes them ideal for forming the phospholipid bilayer.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Carbohydrates — serving as enzymes and structural scaffolds</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Enzymes and structural scaffolds are functions of proteins, the most versatile biomolecule class. Carbohydrates handle energy storage and cell recognition.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Proteins — storing and transferring genetic information</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Genetic information storage and transfer are functions of nucleic acids (DNA and RNA). Proteins serve as enzymes, transporters, and structural scaffolds.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the four biomolecule classes: carbohydrates (energy storage, cell recognition), lipids (membranes, energy, signals), proteins (enzymes, transporters, scaffolds), and nucleic acids (genetic information)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done! Lipids are essential for membrane construction, energy storage, and signal transduction — all critical to cellular organisation."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
