          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>The cell membrane is described as 'fluid' in the fluid mosaic model because:</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>The cell membrane is described as 'fluid' in the fluid mosaic model because:</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Water molecules pass freely through the lipid bilayer without restriction</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Water crosses the membrane mainly through aquaporin channel proteins, not freely through the lipid bilayer. The term 'fluid' refers to the lateral movement of phospholipids within each layer.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cholesterol dissolves in the membrane, making it liquid at body temperature</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Cholesterol acts as a fluidity buffer — it stiffens the membrane at high temperatures and prevents rigidity at low temperatures. 'Fluid' describes the lateral movement of phospholipids.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Integral proteins constantly rotate between the inner and outer layers</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Integral proteins span the bilayer but do not flip between layers. The fluidity refers to phospholipid molecules sliding laterally within their respective layers.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Phospholipid molecules can slide freely within each layer of the bilayer</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! The fluid mosaic model, proposed by Singer and Nicolson in 1972, describes a membrane where phospholipids move laterally within each leaflet, giving the membrane its dynamic, fluid character.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The fluid mosaic model describes a membrane where phospholipids slide freely within each layer (fluid) and proteins are embedded throughout like tiles (mosaic)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right! Phospholipid lateral mobility is what makes the membrane 'fluid,' while the embedded proteins create the 'mosaic' pattern."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
