          "label": "<p>How many sodium and potassium ions does the sodium-potassium pump transport per </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>How many sodium and potassium ions does the sodium-potassium pump transport per cycle of ATP hydrolysis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>2 sodium ions out, 3 potassium ions in</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect — the ratio is reversed. The pump moves 3 sodium ions out and 2 potassium ions in. This 3:2 ratio creates a net positive charge outside the cell.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>3 sodium ions out, 2 potassium ions in</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase moves 3 sodium ions out and 2 potassium ions in per ATP molecule, creating the electrochemical gradient that nerve and muscle cells depend on.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>1 sodium ion out, 1 potassium ion in</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The pump is not a 1:1 exchanger. It moves 3 sodium ions out and 2 potassium ions in per ATP cycle, creating an unequal charge distribution essential for nerve and muscle function.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>4 sodium ions out, 3 potassium ions in</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The correct stoichiometry is 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in per cycle, not 4:3. This specific ratio is essential for maintaining the resting membrane potential.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pumps 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in per ATP cycle. This unequal exchange generates the electrical gradient across cell membranes."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! The 3:2 transport ratio of the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase is fundamental to membrane physiology — the drug digoxin works by inhibiting this pump in cardiac muscle."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
